WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.080
- You won't vote.

00:00:01.080 --> 00:00:02.030
You're not gonna vote.

00:00:02.030 --> 00:00:03.860
- Young voters often being considered

00:00:03.860 --> 00:00:04.960
politically uninterested.

00:00:04.960 --> 00:00:07.010
- Young voters here could
make a big difference.

00:00:07.010 --> 00:00:07.843
- You're not gonna vote.

00:00:07.843 --> 00:00:09.580
Hey, you hear me?

00:00:09.580 --> 00:00:10.413
Why do I know that?

00:00:10.413 --> 00:00:12.910
Because, I'm just using
basic statistics here.

00:00:12.910 --> 00:00:16.180
YouTube tells me that 71% of my audience,

00:00:16.180 --> 00:00:17.560
all of you watching,

00:00:17.560 --> 00:00:20.680
are between the ages of
18 and 34 years old, okay?

00:00:20.680 --> 00:00:23.730
That's me too, I'm 32
years old, so whatever.

00:00:23.730 --> 00:00:25.363
I'm in this demographic.

00:00:26.327 --> 00:00:29.060
We could also be represented on a graph

00:00:29.060 --> 00:00:32.017
that shows how many of
us shows up to the polls

00:00:32.017 --> 00:00:34.450
every election.

00:00:34.450 --> 00:00:39.450
Back in the 80's that
number was around 50%, half.

00:00:39.780 --> 00:00:43.040
And then by the 90's it
dipped down really low,

00:00:43.040 --> 00:00:45.810
to like 40%.

00:00:45.810 --> 00:00:46.960
And then it went up a little,

00:00:46.960 --> 00:00:47.930
and then in 2016,

00:00:47.930 --> 00:00:51.200
the last general election, it was 46%.

00:00:51.200 --> 00:00:54.280
Meanwhile, the older
folks are way up here,

00:00:54.280 --> 00:00:56.920
in like the 60's and 70's.

00:00:56.920 --> 00:01:01.920
People over 65 have almost
50% more representation

00:01:02.573 --> 00:01:06.430
in our democracy, because
simply, they show up to polls

00:01:06.430 --> 00:01:07.920
and they vote.

00:01:07.920 --> 00:01:10.930
Whereas, a lot of us stay home.

00:01:10.930 --> 00:01:12.970
But here's what blows my mind about this,

00:01:12.970 --> 00:01:14.920
as I think about it this year,

00:01:14.920 --> 00:01:16.250
I was this guy.

00:01:16.250 --> 00:01:17.620
Like, I was this guy.

00:01:17.620 --> 00:01:19.330
I was the person who didn't vote.

00:01:19.330 --> 00:01:21.190
Didn't really think about this as some

00:01:21.190 --> 00:01:22.590
super important thing.

00:01:22.590 --> 00:01:24.250
Let's just like rewind

00:01:24.250 --> 00:01:27.060
at me being this guy, in 2012.

00:01:27.060 --> 00:01:28.830
Of course, I was on YouTube

00:01:28.830 --> 00:01:30.760
watching all the celebrities telling me

00:01:30.760 --> 00:01:33.280
that it's my civic duty
to go out and vote.

00:01:33.280 --> 00:01:35.230
- It's only one wrong vote,

00:01:35.230 --> 00:01:36.700
and that is no vote.

00:01:36.700 --> 00:01:38.220
- And they would use
this weird phrase like,

00:01:38.220 --> 00:01:39.522
get out and vote.

00:01:39.522 --> 00:01:40.880
It was like, "Get out, and vote".

00:01:40.880 --> 00:01:43.260
- So, on November 4th, get out and vote.

00:01:43.260 --> 00:01:44.900
- Why are we saying get out all the time?

00:01:44.900 --> 00:01:46.760
So weird, like it's, what a weird phrase.

00:01:46.760 --> 00:01:47.940
We don't use that for anything else.

00:01:47.940 --> 00:01:49.670
I would hear these celebrities
and I would kind of

00:01:49.670 --> 00:01:50.510
roll my eyes.

00:01:50.510 --> 00:01:52.110
Like yeah, I get it, we're in a democracy,

00:01:52.110 --> 00:01:53.170
this is important, but,

00:01:53.170 --> 00:01:54.650
it's not that important.

00:01:54.650 --> 00:01:56.220
And you know what added to all of this

00:01:56.220 --> 00:01:57.580
for me in 2012?

00:01:57.580 --> 00:01:59.250
Is that these ads, these celebrities,

00:01:59.250 --> 00:02:01.060
they made it sound so easy.

00:02:01.060 --> 00:02:03.260
Just like, "Take a couple
minutes and go vote".

00:02:03.260 --> 00:02:05.660
Like, it's actually not that easy.

00:02:05.660 --> 00:02:08.920
Voting in America requires
a bit of research,

00:02:08.920 --> 00:02:09.860
and a bit of effort.

00:02:09.860 --> 00:02:10.693
Not a ton,

00:02:10.693 --> 00:02:14.050
but more than what a lot of
people make it sound like.

00:02:14.050 --> 00:02:16.320
And this is all made
muddier by the fact that

00:02:16.320 --> 00:02:17.890
every state has specific rules.

00:02:17.890 --> 00:02:19.320
I was moving around a lot at this time.

00:02:19.320 --> 00:02:20.930
I was in Oregon, and then I was in Utah,

00:02:20.930 --> 00:02:22.350
and then I was in Virginia.

00:02:22.350 --> 00:02:24.910
And every one of these states
has slightly different rules

00:02:24.910 --> 00:02:26.620
on how to register, when to register,

00:02:26.620 --> 00:02:28.390
how to actually vote.

00:02:28.390 --> 00:02:30.360
Which is why there's a YouTube channel

00:02:30.360 --> 00:02:31.833
literally dedicated to

00:02:31.833 --> 00:02:34.310
how to vote in every state.

00:02:34.310 --> 00:02:35.910
It requires a tutorial.

00:02:35.910 --> 00:02:38.285
So yeah, voting requires a little bit of

00:02:38.285 --> 00:02:41.730
research and effort, and we
should be upfront about that.

00:02:41.730 --> 00:02:44.880
Look at this ranking of 20 democracies

00:02:44.880 --> 00:02:48.080
ranked by how much young
people show up to vote.

00:02:48.080 --> 00:02:50.560
Of course, up here we have
the Scandinavian countries,

00:02:50.560 --> 00:02:53.570
