00:05:35.980 --> 00:05:37.990
Okay, let's get back to voting.

00:05:37.990 --> 00:05:39.380
I'm still not very happy

00:05:39.380 --> 00:05:43.670
about the Switzerland flex
over there from my wife.

00:05:43.670 --> 00:05:45.700
- [Wife] Just admit that it's not perfect.

00:05:45.700 --> 00:05:46.900
- Okay, so back to voting.

00:05:46.900 --> 00:05:50.310
Less than half of people
under 30 don't vote.

00:05:50.310 --> 00:05:53.350
And what does this actually
mean for our society?

00:05:53.350 --> 00:05:54.710
Well, it means that older people

00:05:54.710 --> 00:05:56.450
have way more representation

00:05:56.450 --> 00:05:58.570
to elect other older people,

00:05:58.570 --> 00:06:01.146
who have different
perspectives and priorities

00:06:01.146 --> 00:06:03.070
than younger people do.

00:06:03.070 --> 00:06:04.620
Tax cuts for rich people,

00:06:04.620 --> 00:06:06.800
who tend to be older people, check.

00:06:06.800 --> 00:06:09.260
They usually don't want
things to change very much,

00:06:09.260 --> 00:06:11.010
because they've sorta
been doing it the same way

00:06:11.010 --> 00:06:12.820
for so many years, check.

00:06:12.820 --> 00:06:14.470
The changing climate,

00:06:14.470 --> 00:06:16.560
yeah, they certainly care
about climate change,

00:06:16.560 --> 00:06:18.000
but like, they're not
gonna be on the Earth

00:06:18.000 --> 00:06:19.240
for that much longer.

00:06:19.240 --> 00:06:20.850
So, it's like they don't actually care,

00:06:20.850 --> 00:06:21.870
they don't have skin in the game

00:06:21.870 --> 00:06:23.010
with the climate change thing.

00:06:23.010 --> 00:06:25.210
This is why one election expert said that,

00:06:25.210 --> 00:06:27.200
if younger people showed up to the polls

00:06:27.200 --> 00:06:30.150
in the same proportions as older people,

00:06:30.150 --> 00:06:33.710
it would fundamentally
transform American politics.

00:06:33.710 --> 00:06:35.970
But wait, let's go back to 2012 Johnny,

00:06:35.970 --> 00:06:38.910
and go through another
thing that was in his head.

00:06:38.910 --> 00:06:40.450
Because, one of the big things,

00:06:40.450 --> 00:06:41.960
is I was living in Utah at the time.

00:06:41.960 --> 00:06:43.600
Which is a deeply red state

00:06:43.600 --> 00:06:45.890
that was definitely
gonna go for Mitt Romney,

00:06:45.890 --> 00:06:47.420
and I was an Obama person.

00:06:47.420 --> 00:06:49.120
And so, I was thinking,

00:06:49.120 --> 00:06:50.770
my vote doesn't count.

00:06:50.770 --> 00:06:53.260
Which is actually a very
tempting line of thinking,

00:06:53.260 --> 00:06:56.150
and probably a major reason
why a lot of people don't vote.

00:06:56.150 --> 00:06:59.030
But now that I have studied a
lot of different governments,

00:06:59.030 --> 00:07:01.310
and how things work in
the world, I realize that

00:07:01.310 --> 00:07:04.210
that line of thinking is totally wrong.

00:07:04.210 --> 00:07:06.980
First off, there are loads
of people on that ballot

00:07:06.980 --> 00:07:09.020
that are not running for president,

00:07:09.020 --> 00:07:11.260
that are running for state, and local,

00:07:11.260 --> 00:07:12.417
and county positions,

00:07:12.417 --> 00:07:14.980
that incidentally, have way more effect

00:07:14.980 --> 00:07:16.720
on your day to day life

00:07:16.720 --> 00:07:18.440
than the presidential election.

00:07:18.440 --> 00:07:19.273
Sorry to break it to you,

00:07:19.273 --> 00:07:21.670
but like, presidential
election gets all the limelight

00:07:21.670 --> 00:07:23.950
and attention, actually probably affects

00:07:23.950 --> 00:07:25.630
your life a lot less.

00:07:25.630 --> 00:07:26.463
Anyway.

00:07:26.463 --> 00:07:27.296
Plus that whole idea of,

00:07:27.296 --> 00:07:28.129
my vote doesn't count,

00:07:28.129 --> 00:07:29.730
is not how democracy works.

00:07:29.730 --> 00:07:31.220
Oftentimes democracy feels like

00:07:31.220 --> 00:07:32.490
a winner takes all situation,

00:07:32.490 --> 00:07:33.840
where whoever wins,

00:07:33.840 --> 00:07:36.387
wins and they do whatever
they want, because they won.

00:07:36.387 --> 00:07:38.477
That's not totally the case.

00:07:38.477 --> 00:07:41.670
Even if you vote for a losing candidate,

00:07:41.670 --> 00:07:45.903
democracy is a game of
pressuring and signaling leaders.

00:07:45.903 --> 00:07:47.360
Lemme explain what I mean.

00:07:47.360 --> 00:07:50.660
Leaders of course, take note of protests,

00:07:50.660 --> 00:07:54.150
and petitions, and
other forms of activism.

00:07:54.150 --> 00:07:55.810
But the thing they really care about,

00:07:55.810 --> 00:07:57.730
the thing that really pressures them,

00:07:57.730 --> 00:07:58.563
is the vote.

00:07:58.563 --> 00:08:00.570
Because that is the only
thing that can actually

00:08:00.570 --> 00:08:01.990
lose them their jobs.

00:08:01.990 --> 00:08:04.130
Even if your candidate loses,

00:08:04.130 --> 00:08:06.950
let's say you are a red
person voting in a blue state,

00:08:06.950 --> 00:08:09.020
or a blue person voting in
a red state, or whatever,

00:08:09.020 --> 00:08:11.950
the winner of that
election will still note

00:08:11.950 --> 00:08:13.630
the results of their opponent.

00:08:13.630 --> 00:08:16.050
If there's a huge turnout
for your candidate,

00:08:16.050 --> 00:08:17.040
even though they lost,

00:08:17.040 --> 00:08:19.410
the winner of that election will notice,

00:08:19.410 --> 00:08:20.600
and they will take note,

00:08:20.600 --> 00:08:22.800
and they will factor
that into their behavior,

00:08:22.800 --> 00:08:24.040
and to their decision making.

00:08:24.040 --> 00:08:24.873
That is a fact.

00:08:24.873 --> 00:08:26.210
That is how democracy works.

00:08:26.210 --> 00:08:27.400
Because they need to keep an eye on it,

00:08:27.400 --> 00:08:29.840
because they realize that
if there was a big turnout

00:08:29.840 --> 00:08:31.680
for their opponent this time,

00:08:31.680 --> 00:08:32.946
they need to keep an eye on that,

00:08:32.946 --> 00:08:34.270
for the next election.

00:08:34.270 --> 00:08:35.103
On the other hand,

00:08:35.103 --> 00:08:36.650
if you don't vote and you stay home,

00:08:36.650 --> 00:08:37.730
that candidate who won,

00:08:37.730 --> 00:08:39.210
has zero pressure.

00:08:39.210 --> 00:08:41.980
They feel zero pressure
to behave any differently,

00:08:41.980 --> 00:08:44.050
because they won so comfortably.

00:08:44.050 --> 00:08:46.570
Voting isn't just about
voting for the winner,

00:08:46.570 --> 00:08:48.534
it's about signaling to everyone,

00:08:48.534 --> 00:08:50.995
what the actual political diversity is,

00:08:50.995 --> 00:08:52.776
in your city, or your county,

00:08:52.776 --> 00:08:54.700
or your state or your country.

00:08:54.700 --> 00:08:56.480
Your vote actually does matter,

00:08:56.480 --> 00:08:58.100
even when it feels like it doesn't.

00:08:58.100 --> 00:08:59.396
And this took me a while to like,

00:08:59.396 --> 00:09:01.610
actually accept,

00:09:01.610 --> 00:09:03.300
but now I see it very clearly.

00:09:03.300 --> 00:09:04.630
Not only in our democracy,

00:09:04.630 --> 00:09:06.150
but in democracies around the world.

00:09:06.150 --> 00:09:07.520
Okay, I know I sound like I'm sort of

00:09:07.520 --> 00:09:08.750
getting all lecturey here,

00:09:08.750 --> 00:09:11.800
there are a lot of really
good, rational reasons

00:09:11.800 --> 00:09:13.190
why young people don't vote.

00:09:13.190 --> 00:09:15.780
Often, we have less flexible jobs,

00:09:15.780 --> 00:09:19.267
so it isn't easy for us to
take time out to go vote,

00:09:19.267 --> 00:09:21.840
and it can cost us a
lot of money to do that.

00:09:21.840 --> 00:09:23.210
Often we haven't made a habit out of it.

00:09:23.210 --> 00:09:25.690
People that are over 65 have
been doing this for a while.

00:09:25.690 --> 00:09:27.540
They've had time to develop the habit,

00:09:27.540 --> 00:09:29.173
and get used to the experience of voting,

00:09:29.173 --> 00:09:31.390
and honestly, that positive association

00:09:31.390 --> 00:09:33.420
of going to the polls, voting,

00:09:33.420 --> 00:09:34.740
feeling good about it,

00:09:34.740 --> 00:09:36.367
and then the next time you're like,

00:09:36.367 --> 00:09:37.890
"Oh I wanna do that again".

00:09:37.890 --> 00:09:40.170
Young people haven't had the
experience of doing that yet.

00:09:40.170 --> 00:09:42.990
And listen, I don't think this
video's gonna suddenly change

00:09:42.990 --> 00:09:45.010
this line, and just
have it start to go up,

00:09:45.010 --> 00:09:48.220
and suddenly we have a 70%
turnout for young people,

00:09:48.220 --> 00:09:49.680
though that would be really like psh,

00:09:49.680 --> 00:09:50.700
change our entire country.

00:09:50.700 --> 00:09:52.870
But I do think something is changing.

00:09:52.870 --> 00:09:55.910
I think us young people are realizing that

00:09:55.910 --> 00:09:58.395
a lot of real, serious threats

00:09:58.395 --> 00:10:02.330
are being created and
exacerbated by the old folks

00:10:02.330 --> 00:10:04.500
that have been running things
for a really long time.

00:10:04.500 --> 00:10:08.400
And that those threats could
actually have huge affects

00:10:08.400 --> 00:10:11.953
on our future, and our
future job prospects,

00:10:11.953 --> 00:10:13.820
what our society looks like.

00:10:13.820 --> 00:10:16.160
And I think we're realizing that yeah,

00:10:16.160 --> 00:10:18.430
even though you have to
do a little research,

00:10:18.430 --> 00:10:20.230
and that voting laws
are a little different

00:10:20.230 --> 00:10:22.130
in every state, that
it's worth the effort.

00:10:22.130 --> 00:10:24.090
Even if our candidate loses,

00:10:24.090 --> 00:10:27.030
to send a signal about what we want

00:10:27.030 --> 00:10:29.742
and what we care about in our democracy.

00:10:29.742 --> 00:10:32.325
(upbeat music)

